<script>
  // ES modules are blocked on file:// — without this the page would just sit
  // black with a CORS error in a console nobody opened.
  if (!(window.WebAssembly && document.createElement('canvas').getContext('webgl2'))) {
    document.getElementById('boot').innerHTML =
      '<div class="boot-inner"><h2>This build did not load</h2>' +
      '<p class="hint">Every module in this page is inline, so nothing was fetched and nothing can 404. A browser with WebGL2 and WebAssembly runs it; one without either stops here.</p></div>';
  }
</script>
<!--
  THE TOUCH PAD, and it is EMPTY until a finger lands on the page.

  "the game actually runs pretty decent on the phone but there's no control
  scheme… is there any way you can make touch controls for the phone? (Don't
  fuck up the actual desktop game)"

  Every node inside it is built by `src/engine/Touch.js` on the first real
  `touchstart` and never before, so a desktop — including a laptop with a
  touchscreen — has an empty div and one passive listener, which is the whole
  of what this feature costs a mouse. The container is here rather than being
  created in JS for the reason every other overlay in this file is: a surface
  the stylesheet has to place cannot be a string inside a module.

  OUTSIDE #hud, because it is not part of the readout — it is the controller.
  The HUD is hidden on the menu and on every overlay; the pad is hidden with it
  through `Touch.show`, which also lets go of anything a thumb was holding.
-->
<div id="touch" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<script type="module" >import "m:src/main.js";</script>
